# History: mcp-tape, blind build r05. mcp-tape's decision D-026 described a Windows trap as
# context: a Python child's `print` writes CRLF on Windows, so a test that expects LF from it
# fails there. r05 skipped its POSIX-only tests on Windows as REQ-IF-001 asked, but one of its
# tests still expected LF from a Python child. D-028 then moved the fact into REQ-IF-001:
# "A builder acts on requirements and reads context."
#
# duramen rejects RFC 2119 keywords outside requirements, decisions included (T004). Written as
# the decision below, the fact has to move into a `req`, where it gets an ID and needs an
# evaluation. D-026 as written had no uppercase keyword (only a lowercase "must", twice, about
# other rules), so no check in duramen would have flagged it. This file shows the form of the
# mistake a checker can see.
duramen 0.1

spec mcp-tape-r05 1.1.1

decision D-026 "The builder's own tests run on both platforms"
  source r04 (a check on Windows, outside the suite)
  text
    A Python child's `print` writes CRLF on Windows, so a test that compares bytes a child
    wrote MUST have the child write bytes, or accept CRLF.
